 "text": "### Problem (a): Bubble-point temperature of the feed at 120 kPa\n\n**Step 1: Convert mass fractions to mole fractions**\nThe mass fractions are given as \\( w_1 = 0.30 \\), \\( w_2 = 0.40 \\), \\( w_3 = 0.30 \\). The molar masses are \\( M_1 = 78.11 \\), \\( M_2 = 92.14 \\), \\( M_3 = 106.17 \\) g/mol.\n\nThe mole fraction \\( x_i \\) is calculated as:\n\\[ x_i = \\frac{w_i / M_i}{\\sum (w_i / M_i)} \\]\n\nCalculate the denominator:\n\\[ \\sum (w_i / M_i) = \\frac{0.30}{78.11} + \\frac{0.40}{92.14} + \\frac{0.30}{106.17} \\]\n\\[ = 0.003840 + 0.004341 + 0.002826 = 0.011007 \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( x_i \\):\n\\[ x_1 = \\frac{0.003840}{0.011007} = 0.3489 \\]\n\\[ x_2 = \\frac{0.004341}{0.011007} = 0.3944 \\]\n\\[ x_3 = \\frac{0.002826}{0.011007} = 0.2567 \\]\n\n**Step 2: Bubble-point condition**\nAt the bubble point, \\( \\sum y_i = \\sum K_i x_i = 1 \\), where \\( K_i = P_i^{sat} / P \\).\n\nThe total pressure \\( P = 120 \\) kPa = \\( 120 / 0.133322 = 900 \\) mmHg.\n\nThe bubble-point equation is:\n\\[ \\sum K_i x_i = \\sum \\frac{P_i^{sat}}{900} x_i = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 900 \\]\n\n**Step 3: Solve for temperature \\( T \\)**\nWe need to find \\( T \\) such that \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 900 \\). We will use the Antoine equation to calculate \\( P_i^{sat} \\):\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_i^{sat} = A_i - \\frac{B_i}{C_i + T} \\]\n\\[ P_i^{sat} = 10^{A_i - B_i / (C_i + T)} \\]\n\nWe will use trial and error to find \\( T \\). Let's start with \\( T = 100 \\)\u00b0C:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 100} = 6.90565 - 3.770 = 3.13565 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.13565} = 1367.5 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 100} = 6.95464 - 4.199 = 2.75564 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{2.75564} = 569.8 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 100} = 6.99891 - 4.698 = 2.30091 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.30091} = 200.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i \\):\n\\[ \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 1367.5 \\times 0.3489 + 569.8 \\times 0.3944 + 200.0 \\times 0.2567 \\]\n\\[ = 477.1 + 224.7 + 51.3 = 753.1 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nThis is less than 900 mmHg, so we need a higher temperature. Let's try \\( T = 110 \\)\u00b0C:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 110} = 6.90565 - 3.653 = 3.25265 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.25265} = 1790.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 110} = 6.95464 - 4.065 = 2.88964 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{2.88964} = 775.6 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 110} = 6.99891 - 4.540 = 2.45891 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.45891} = 287.7 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i \\):\n\\[ \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 1790.0 \\times 0.3489 + 775.6 \\times 0.3944 + 287.7 \\times 0.2567 \\]\n\\[ = 624.5 + 306.0 + 73.8 = 1004.3 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nThis is greater than 900 mmHg, so the bubble point is between 100\u00b0C and 110\u00b0C. Let's try \\( T = 105 \\)\u00b0C:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 105} = 6.90565 - 3.710 = 3.19565 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.19565} = 1570.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 105} = 6.95464 - 4.130 = 2.82464 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{2.82464} = 667.8 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 105} = 6.99891 - 4.617 = 2.38191 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.38191} = 240.9 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i \\):\n\\[ \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 1570.0 \\times 0.3489 + 667.8 \\times 0.3944 + 240.9 \\times 0.2567 \\]\n\\[ = 547.8 + 263.3 + 61.8 = 872.9 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nThis is slightly less than 900 mmHg. Let's try \\( T = 106 \\)\u00b0C:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 106} = 6.90565 - 3.695 = 3.21065 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.21065} = 1624.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 106} = 6.95464 - 4.116 = 2.83864 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{2.83864} = 690.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 106} = 6.99891 - 4.603 = 2.39591 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.39591} = 248.9 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i \\):\n\\[ \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 1624.0 \\times 0.3489 + 690.0 \\times 0.3944 + 248.9 \\times 0.2567 \\]\n\\[ = 566.6 + 272.1 + 63.9 = 902.6 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nThis is very close to 900 mmHg. The bubble-point temperature is approximately 106\u00b0C.\n\n**Interpolation for better precision:**\nAt \\( T = 105 \\)\u00b0C, \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 872.9 \\) mmHg.\nAt \\( T = 106 \\)\u00b0C, \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 902.6 \\) mmHg.\n\nWe need \\( \\sum P_i^{sat} x_i = 900 \\) mmHg:\n\\[ T = 105 + \\frac{900 - 872.9}{902.6 - 872.9} = 105 + \\frac{27.1}{29.7} = 105 + 0.912 = 105.912 \\text{\u00b0C} \\]\n\nFINAL (a): 105.91 \u00b0C\n\n---\n\n### Problem (b): Dew-point temperature of the feed at 120 kPa\n\n**Step 1: Dew-point condition**\nAt the dew point, \\( \\sum x_i = \\sum \\frac{y_i}{K_i} = 1 \\), where \\( y_i = z_i \\) (feed mole fraction) and \\( K_i = P_i^{sat} / P \\).\n\nThe dew-point equation is:\n\\[ \\sum \\frac{y_i}{K_i} = \\sum \\frac{y_i P}{P_i^{sat}} = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} = \\frac{1}{P} = \\frac{1}{900} \\]\n\n**Step 2: Solve for temperature \\( T \\)**\nWe need to find \\( T \\) such that \\( \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} = \\frac{1}{900} \\). We will use the Antoine equation as before.\n\nLet's start with \\( T = 120 \\)\u00b0C:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 120} = 6.90565 - 3.547 = 3.35865 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.35865} = 2283.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 120} = 6.95464 - 3.938 = 3.01664 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{3.01664} = 1039.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 120} = 6.99891 - 4.396 = 2.60291 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.60291} = 400.8 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} \\):\n\\[ \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} = \\frac{0.3489}{2283.0} + \\frac{0.3944}{1039.0} + \\frac{0.2567}{400.8} \\]\n\\[ = 0.0001528 + 0.0003796 + 0.0006405 = 0.0011729 \\]\n\nThis is greater than \\( \\frac{1}{900} = 0.0011111 \\), so we need a higher temperature. Let's try \\( T = 125 \\)\u00b0C:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 125} = 6.90565 - 3.485 = 3.42065 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.42065} = 2634.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 125} = 6.95464 - 3.878 = 3.07664 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{3.07664} = 1193.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 125} = 6.99891 - 4.340 = 2.65891 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.65891} = 455.9 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} \\):\n\\[ \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} = \\frac{0.3489}{2634.0} + \\frac{0.3944}{1193.0} + \\frac{0.2567}{455.9} \\]\n\\[ = 0.0001325 + 0.0003306 + 0.0005631 = 0.0010262 \\]\n\nThis is less than \\( \\frac{1}{900} \\), so the dew point is between 120\u00b0C and 125\u00b0C. Let's try \\( T = 122 \\)\u00b0C:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 122} = 6.90565 - 3.519 = 3.38665 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.38665} = 2436.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 122} = 6.95464 - 3.911 = 3.04364 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{3.04364} = 1106.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 122} = 6.99891 - 4.371 = 2.62791 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.62791} = 424.5 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} \\):\n\\[ \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} = \\frac{0.3489}{2436.0} + \\frac{0.3944}{1106.0} + \\frac{0.2567}{424.5} \\]\n\\[ = 0.0001432 + 0.0003566 + 0.0006047 = 0.0011045 \\]\n\nThis is very close to \\( \\frac{1}{900} = 0.0011111 \\). The dew-point temperature is approximately 122\u00b0C.\n\n**Interpolation for better precision:**\nAt \\( T = 122 \\)\u00b0C, \\( \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} = 0.0011045 \\).\nAt \\( T = 121 \\)\u00b0C, let's calculate \\( \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} \\):\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 121} = 6.90565 - 3.533 = 3.37265 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.37265} = 2358.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 121} = 6.95464 - 3.924 = 3.03064 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{3.03064} = 1073.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 121} = 6.99891 - 4.383 = 2.61591 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.61591} = 413.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} \\):\n\\[ \\sum \\frac{y_i}{P_i^{sat}} = \\frac{0.3489}{2358.0} + \\frac{0.3944}{1073.0} + \\frac{0.2567}{413.0} \\]\n\\[ = 0.0001480 + 0.0003676 + 0.0006215 = 0.0011371 \\]\n\nInterpolate:\n\\[ T = 121 + \\frac{0.0011111 - 0.0011371}{0.0011045 - 0.0011371} = 121 + \\frac{-0.0000260}{-0.0000326} = 121 + 0.798 = 121.798 \\text{\u00b0C} \\]\n\nFINAL (b): 121.80 \u00b0C\n\n---\n\n### Problem (c): Vapour fraction \\( V/F \\) at 112\u00b0C and 120 kPa\n\n**Step 1: Calculate \\( K_i \\) at 112\u00b0C**\nUsing the Antoine equation:\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_1^{sat} = 6.90565 - \\frac{1211.033}{220.790 + 112} = 6.90565 - 3.678 = 3.22765 \\]\n\\[ P_1^{sat} = 10^{3.22765} = 1689.0 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\\[ K_1 = \\frac{1689.0}{900} = 1.8767 \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_2^{sat} = 6.95464 - \\frac{1344.800}{219.482 + 112} = 6.95464 - 4.092 = 2.86264 \\]\n\\[ P_2^{sat} = 10^{2.86264} = 728.9 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\\[ K_2 = \\frac{728.9}{900} = 0.8099 \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\log_{10} P_3^{sat} = 6.99891 - \\frac{1474.679}{213.686 + 112} = 6.99891 - 4.570 = 2.42891 \\]\n\\[ P_3^{sat} = 10^{2.42891} = 268.5 \\text{ mmHg} \\]\n\\[ K_3 = \\frac{268.5}{900} = 0.2983 \\]\n\n**Step 2: Flash equation**\nThe flash equation is:\n\\[ \\sum \\frac{z_i (K_i - 1)}{1 + \\psi (K_i - 1)} = 0 \\]\nwhere \\( \\psi = V/F \\).\n\nWe need to solve for \\( \\psi \\). Let's try \\( \\psi = 0.5 \\):\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.3489 (1.8767 - 1)}{1 + 0.5 (1.8767 - 1)} = \\frac{0.3489 \\times 0.8767}{1 + 0.5 \\times 0.8767} = \\frac{0.3059}{1.4384} = 0.2127 \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.3944 (0.8099 - 1)}{1 + 0.5 (0.8099 - 1)} = \\frac{0.3944 \\times (-0.1901)}{1 + 0.5 \\times (-0.1901)} = \\frac{-0.0749}{0.9049} = -0.0828 \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.2567 (0.2983 - 1)}{1 + 0.5 (0.2983 - 1)} = \\frac{0.2567 \\times (-0.7017)}{1 + 0.5 \\times (-0.7017)} = \\frac{-0.1801}{0.6492} = -0.2774 \\]\n\nSum:\n\\[ 0.2127 - 0.0828 - 0.2774 = -0.1475 \\]\n\nThis is negative, so \\( \\psi \\) should be less than 0.5. Let's try \\( \\psi = 0.3 \\):\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.3489 \\times 0.8767}{1 + 0.3 \\times 0.8767} = \\frac{0.3059}{1.2630} = 0.2422 \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.3944 \\times (-0.1901)}{1 + 0.3 \\times (-0.1901)} = \\frac{-0.0749}{0.9429} = -0.0795 \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.2567 \\times (-0.7017)}{1 + 0.3 \\times (-0.7017)} = \\frac{-0.1801}{0.7895} = -0.2281 \\]\n\nSum:\n\\[ 0.2422 - 0.0795 - 0.2281 = -0.0654 \\]\n\nStill negative. Let's try \\( \\psi = 0.2 \\):\n\nFor benzene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.3489 \\times 0.8767}{1 + 0.2 \\times 0.8767} = \\frac{0.3059}{1.1753} = 0.2603 \\]\n\nFor toluene:\n\\[ \\frac{0.3944 \\times (-0.1901)}{1 + 0.2 \\times (-0.1901)} = \\frac{-0.0749}{0.9619} = -0.0779 \\]\n\nFor o-xylene:\n\\[ \\frac{0